\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}{{1.1}{9}{Place holder graphic. Caption to the effect of... The LO beam enters HAM6 directly above BHDBS2 (north side), and it is highlighted in green. A pick-off will be used to monitor the alignment of the LO; the pick-off beam is indicated with a dashed green line. The signal beam enters HAM6 directly above OM0 (north side), and it is highlighted in pink. The signal beam is steered by OM0 onto BHDBS2 where it is combined with the local oscillator. The shutter is located between OM0 and BHDBS2. A small amount of the signal beam is transmitted by OM0 (dashed pink); this beam is used align \ref {}. The two combined beams are shown in red. These are incident upon a mirror used for steering (OMxS) and then upon two mirrors curved mirrors (OMx1 and OMx2). (Caption continued on next page).\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces (Figure on previous page). The BHSS (the large triangular component) takes up most of the bottom left of the HAM6 chamber. The position of the OMCs is shown. On the BHSS, there are several small optics used to steer beams that are required to control \ref  {}; the wavefront sensors (yellow components) for this are found at the top of the HAM6 table. Several beams are directed of the table for imaging with CCD cameras.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.9}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Astigmatism is a source of loss. When designing the layout shown in Figure \ref  {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}, the AOI for the curved mirrors was altered to explore how sensitive it was to astigmatism. Using Equation (\ref  {eq:bhd_for_ligo_mm_loss}), the loss was computed. This effect is negligible up to 10\si {\degree } after which it starts to become significant compared to the losses summarised in Table \ref  {tab:HAM6_losses}.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.10}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterastigmatism}{{1.2}{10}{Astigmatism is a source of loss. When designing the layout shown in Figure \ref {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}, the AOI for the curved mirrors was altered to explore how sensitive it was to astigmatism. Using Equation (\ref {eq:bhd_for_ligo_mm_loss}), the loss was computed. This effect is negligible up to 10\si {\degree } after which it starts to become significant compared to the losses summarised in Table \ref {tab:HAM6_losses}.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
